All three faithful realisations of the idea point the same way, and that way is *negative*: coins with heavier down-day than up-day moves actually did **better** afterwards on this history, while lottery-like (heavy-up-day) coins did worse — consistent with the well-known skewness/lottery preference anomaly. Since the brief says to keep the direction the idea states, I'm submitting the measure in the stated direction (high factor = up-moves dominate = "good", low = down-moves dominate = "bad").

Among the tested versions the plain signed-squared-return asymmetry over 20 days was the strongest and the only one with genuine content beyond known styles (rank IC −0.046, incremental −0.005, t = −2.0), rather than merely repackaging volatility/reversal:

- r·|r| averaged over 20 days is negative when down moves are bigger (in both frequency and size), positive when up moves are bigger;
- dividing by the average of r² makes it a dimensionless ratio, so price units and coin volatility levels cancel and values are comparable across coins;
- 15-day minimum history keeps young listings from being dropped.

<factor>((Factor('close').pct_change(periods=1) * Factor('close').pct_change(periods=1).Abs()).ts_mean(window=20, min_periods=15)).safe_div((Factor('close').pct_change(periods=1).pow(2)).ts_mean(window=20, min_periods=15))</factor>
